<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amangkurat IV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1726</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amangkurat IV was ruler of Mataram from 1719 to 1726, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakubuwana-i">Pakubuwana I</a>. His accession led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-javanese-war-of-succession">Second Javanese War of Succession</a>, in which his brothers and uncles rebelled and were defeated with the help of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> by 1723. He then ruled a stable but indebted kingdom bound by Company treaties.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amangkurat IV, also known as Sunan Jawi, was born Raden Mas Suryaputra, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakubuwana-i">Pakubuwana I</a> by his queen consort, Ratu Mas Balitar, a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panembahan-senapati">Panembahan Senapati</a>. His father, as Pangeran Puger, had fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semarang">Semarang</a> in 1704 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-iii">Amangkurat III</a> sent troops to arrest him, and with the backing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartasura">Kartasura</a> in 1705 and became king. In return for that help Pakubuwana I ceded Semarang and its surroundings to the Company.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On his father's death in 1719 Suryaputra took the title Amangkurat IV, which his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-iii">Amangkurat III</a> had borne, rather than Pakubuwana. Discontent in the royal family split the Javanese into five factions: his own and those of his brothers Pangeran Purbaya, Pangeran Balitar and Arya Dipanagara and of his uncle Pangeran Arya Mataram. Balitar restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-agung">Sultan Agung</a>'s old palace as Kartasekar and proclaimed himself sultan, Dipanagara became Panembahan Herucakra at Sukowati, and Arya Mataram attacked towns from the north coast.</p><p>In October 1719 Mataram and Company troops moved against Arya Mataram, who was captured and hanged at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jepara">Jepara</a>. In November 1720 they destroyed Kartasekar, and Balitar died of disease in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malang">Malang</a> in 1721. The war ended in 1723 with the capture of the last leaders: Purbaya was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a>, Dipanagara to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a> and Panji Surengrana to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Amangkurat IV quarrelled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cakraningrat-iv">Cakraningrat IV</a>, regent of West Madura, who believed Madura would prosper under the Company rather than Mataram; relations improved when Cakraningrat married into the royal family. In March 1726 the king fell ill, reportedly poisoned, and he died on 20 April 1726 before the culprit was found. His fifteen-year-old son succeeded as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakubuwono-ii">Pakubuwana II</a>, whose reign brought further conflict ending in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-giyanti">Treaty of Giyanti</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-salatiga">Treaty of Salatiga</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amangkurat IV of Mataram” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
